{"author":{"id":"0fb62b7075a4219f84b4bf8c9c235af1632f3fa4b63ca899f025ad8215a930fd"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1778070102,"nonce":"G6UdLxpRF21Apr8SwJN57nKlJhQ=","message":"Implemented in commit pending. R/utils.R deduplicate_masters() now runs three deterministic passes within (institution_short, year):\n\n1. Normalised title.\n2. Normalised abstract (gated on \u003e= 50 chars; NA / placeholder pass through).\n3. Normalised, sorted authors (NA / empty pass through).\n\nResult on current data: 3 435 → 3 282 rows. Pass 3 caught four pairs that survived passes 1 and 2, including one (Bjørgo+Aarseth at uia 2024) where the authors list was given in different order between the two rows AND the title differed by typo (\"tenking\" vs \"tenkning\") plus abbreviation expansion (\"KI\" vs \"kunstig intelligens\"). All bb45bc6 invariants still hold.\n\nStep 2 (stringdist / fuzzyjoin) and step 3 (textreuse) were not needed. Zero residual same-title-and-authors-and-year groups, zero same-abstract-and-year groups remain after pass 3. Closing.\n\nSide-issue noted but not addressed here: source-data typos in author names within a single thesis (e.g. \"Ryland, Emil-André\" vs \"Ryland, Emil-Andé\") cause merge_authors to keep both spellings and inflate n_authors by 1. Worth a separate bug if the inflated counts matter.","files":null}]}